(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["phy-11-10"] = {"14.1":{"s":"Semiconductors are materials whose electrical properties lie between those of conductors and insulators, and can be tailored by adding impurities. Almost every electronic gadget, from a mobile phone to a satellite, depends on them. This chapter covers how solids conduct, intrinsic and extrinsic semiconductors, the p-n junction diode and its uses, and the thermistor."},"14.2":{"s":"Solids are conductors, insulators or semiconductors according to how many free carriers they have. In a solid the energy levels of the atoms spread into bands. The valence band is the top filled band and the conduction band is the empty one above it; the gap between them is the energy gap. In a metal the bands overlap. In an insulator the gap is large (diamond 5.47 eV). In a semiconductor it is small (silicon 1.12 eV, germanium 0.66 eV).","f":[{"x":"1 eV = 1.6*10^-19 J","n":"The electron-volt: the energy gained by an electron moving through a potential difference of one volt.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 14.2, p.245"}],"w":["The energy gap of a semiconductor is small enough for heat to lift a few electrons across it; an insulator's gap is not."]},"14.4":{"s":"A pure semiconductor, such as silicon or germanium, is called intrinsic. At absolute zero all its valence electrons are in covalent bonds and it does not conduct. At room temperature heat breaks a few bonds. Each broken bond frees an electron and leaves a hole, which behaves like a positive charge. Both electrons and holes carry current, and their numbers are equal.","f":[{"x":"n_e = n_h","n":"Intrinsic semiconductor: the number density of free electrons equals that of holes.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 14.4, p.246"}],"w":["A hole is the absence of an electron in a bond; the current in the same direction is carried by holes moving one way and electrons the other."]},"14.5":{"s":"Adding a small amount of impurity (doping) greatly increases the conductivity of a semiconductor. A pentavalent dopant such as phosphorus, arsenic or antimony gives an n-type semiconductor, where electrons are the majority carriers. A trivalent dopant such as boron, aluminium or indium gives a p-type semiconductor, where holes are the majority carriers. Both types are electrically neutral, even though they have more carriers of one kind.","f":[{"x":"n_e >> n_h (n-type), n_h >> n_e (p-type)","n":"Majority carriers in doped semiconductors: electrons in n-type, holes in p-type.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 14.5, p.247"}],"w":["An n-type crystal is not negatively charged: the donor atoms left behind carry the positive charge that balances the extra electrons."]},"14.7":{"s":"A p-n junction is a p-type and an n-type crystal joined. At the junction electrons and holes diffuse across, leaving a depletion region with no free carriers and a built-in potential barrier. In forward bias (p to the positive terminal) the barrier is lowered and a current of milliamperes flows. In reverse bias the barrier grows and only a tiny current flows. So a diode conducts in one direction only.","f":[{"x":"R_d = V/I","n":"Static (DC) resistance of a diode at a point of its I-V curve.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 14.7, p.254"},{"x":"r_d = delta V/delta I","n":"Dynamic (AC) resistance: the reciprocal of the slope of the I-V curve at that point.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 14.7, p.254"}],"w":["An ideal diode has zero resistance in forward bias and infinite resistance in reverse bias.","Forward bias lowers the potential barrier and narrows the depletion region; reverse bias does the opposite."]},"14.8":{"s":"Semiconductor devices are small and light, need little power and are fast, because they have no filament that must heat up and they work at low voltage. They are also thermally stable and easy to make into integrated circuits. Their disadvantages are that they are sensitive to temperature and radiation, cannot handle high power, are affected by electrostatic charge, and need controlled conditions to manufacture."},"14.9":{"s":"Semiconductor devices have many uses. A solar cell turns light into electricity. A photo resistor changes resistance with light. A photodiode conducts when illuminated. A transistor amplifies and switches. LEDs and solid state lasers emit light with little power. Integrated circuits hold thousands of diodes and transistors on one chip and work in almost all electronic devices."},"14.10":{"s":"A thermistor is a temperature-sensitive resistor made from a metal-oxide semiconductor, whose resistance changes strongly with temperature. In a negative temperature coefficient (NTC) thermistor the resistance falls as the temperature rises; in a positive coefficient (PTC) thermistor it rises. Thermistors can measure very small temperature changes and are used in temperature sensors, and PTC types as resettable fuses.","w":["NTC thermistor: resistance decreases when heated. PTC thermistor: resistance increases when heated."]}};
